      "text": "We are given that Ray climbs stairs of \\( n \\) steps in two different patterns when climbing \\( 4 \\) steps at a time and \\( 5 \\) steps at a time. Let's denote the number of steps to the top as \\( k \\). According to the problem:\n\n1. When Ray climbs \\( 4 \\) steps at a time, there are \\( 3 \\) steps left. This can be expressed as:\n   \\[\n   n \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}\n   \\]\n2. When Ray climbs \\( 5 \\) steps at a time, there are \\( 2 \\) steps left. This can be expressed as:\n   \\[\n   n \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}\n   \\]\n\nWe need to find the smallest \\( n \\) that satisfies both congruences and is greater than \\( 10 \\). To do this, we solve the system of congruences:\n\\[\nn \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}\n\\]\n\\[\nn \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\n\nFirst, express \\( n \\) in terms of the first congruence:\n\\[\nn = 4a + 3 \\quad \\text{for some integer } a\n\\]\n\nSubstitute this expression into the second congruence:\n\\[\n4a + 3 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\n\nSimplify this congruence:\n\\[\n4a + 3 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\n\\[\n4a \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\n\\[\n4a \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5} \\quad \\text{(since } -1 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5} \\text{)}\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\( a \\), divide both sides by \\( 4 \\). Note that \\( 4 \\) has an inverse modulo \\( 5 \\), which is \\( 4 \\) (since \\( 4 \\times 4 = 16 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5} \\)):\n\\[\na \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\n\\[\na = 5b + 4 \\quad \\text{for some integer } b\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\( a = 5b + 4 \\) back into the expression for \\( n \\):\n\\[\nn = 4(5b + 4) + 3\n\\]\n\\[\nn = 20b + 16 + 3\n\\]\n\\[\nn = 20b + 19\n\\]\n\nWe need the smallest \\( n \\) greater than \\( 10 \\). Set \\( b = 0 \\):\n\\[\nn = 20(0) + 19\n\\]\n\\[\nn = 19\n\\]\n\nThus, the smallest possible value of \\( n \\) that satisfies both congruences and is greater than \\( 10 \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{19}\n\\]",
