      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the smallest value of \\( n \\) greater than 10 that satisfies the given conditions:\n1. When Ray climbs the stairs 4 steps at a time, there are 3 steps left at the top.\n2. When Ray climbs the stairs 5 steps at a time, there are 2 steps left at the top.\n\nWe can translate these conditions into congruences:\n1. \\( n \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4} \\)\n2. \\( n \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\)\n\nWe need to find the smallest \\( n > 10 \\) that satisfies both congruences. Let's start by finding a number that satisfies the first congruence, \\( n \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4} \\). This means \\( n = 4k + 3 \\) for some integer \\( k \\).\n\nNext, we substitute \\( n = 4k + 3 \\) into the second congruence \\( n \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\):\n\\[ 4k + 3 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\\[ 4k \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSince \\(-1 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5}\\), we can rewrite the congruence as:\n\\[ 4k \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5} \\]\nWe can divide both sides by 4, noting that 4 and 5 are coprime, so 4 has a multiplicative inverse modulo 5, which is 4. Therefore, we get:\n\\[ k \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5} \\]\nThis means \\( k = 5m + 1 \\) for some integer \\( m \\). Substituting \\( k = 5m + 1 \\) back into the expression for \\( n \\), we get:\n\\[ n = 4(5m + 1) + 3 = 20m + 4 + 3 = 20m + 7 \\]\nSo, \\( n \\) can be written as \\( n = 20m + 7 \\). We need the smallest value of \\( n \\) that is greater than 10. We set \\( m = 0 \\):\n\\[ n = 20 \\cdot 0 + 7 = 7 \\]\nHowever, 7 is not greater than 10. We try \\( m = 1 \\):\n\\[ n = 20 \\cdot 1 + 7 = 27 \\]\nSince 27 is greater than 10, we check if it satisfies both original congruences:\n1. \\( 27 \\div 4 = 6 \\) remainder \\( 3 \\), so \\( 27 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4} \\).\n2. \\( 27 \\div 5 = 5 \\) remainder \\( 2 \\), so \\( 27 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\).\nBoth conditions are satisfied, so the smallest possible value of \\( n \\) that is greater than 10 is:\n\\[ \\boxed{27} \\]",
